WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.369
Our first guest tonight
is an Oscar-winning actor

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:04.703
you know from movies like,

00:00:04.703 --> 00:00:06.673
"Three Billboards Outside
Ebbing, Missouri," and "Vice."

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.975
He just received
a Tony nomination

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:11.410
for Best Leading Actor
in a play for his work

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.747
in "American Buffalo,"
which is playing on Broadway

00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:15.281
at the
Circle in the Square Theatre.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:18.084
Please welcome back to the show
Sam Rockwell, everybody.

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:20.052 line:0%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:28.728 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:30.930
How are you?
-Hey, man. Good, man.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.632
Really good. Hi.

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:34.901
-You know, I realize...

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:36.402
Obviously this is done
for a reason,

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:39.339
because Tony Award nominations
come out on a Monday.

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:40.873
-Yes.
-So all you guys are dark.

00:00:40.873 --> 00:00:42.809
So you can actually celebrate
a little bit.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.010
-That's right. That's right.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.711
I had --
I had a couple of libations,

00:00:45.711 --> 00:00:48.915
and then I got the call about
being on national TV tonight,

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:52.084
and I realized maybe I should
put that 17th one down.

00:00:52.084 --> 00:00:54.887
And I was like,
"Okay. That's enough of that."

00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:57.490
-Yeah.
-But I saw a movie yesterday

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.959
that was -- that I think
your producer was talking about.

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:01.293
-Yeah.

00:01:01.293 --> 00:01:03.963
-"Everything Everywhere
at Once."

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:05.431
-Yes, which I've heard
is incredible.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:07.233
-It's -- I think it's
the best movie this year.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:08.868
I just flipped out, man.

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:10.636
I just thought --
I thought it was just amazing.

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:12.171
-And did you actually
go to the theater?

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:13.540
-I did.
-That's really exciting.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:14.940
-With some friends. Yeah.
It was great.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.109
It was really fun.
-Did you know in the morning --

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:18.845
Did you have any expectation
that the --

00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:20.513
Were you, like,
going to pay attention

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.681
or were you going to wait
until somebody called you,

00:01:22.681 --> 00:01:24.784
as far as the nomination?

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:26.953
-I kind of needed to sleep,
so I did that.

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.955
And then I got
a bunch of texts, yeah.

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:31.323
-Yeah. That's really exciting.
-It is exciting.

00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:33.460
-And tonight, after this,
you're going to go

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.896
and you're going to do your
first show as a Tony nominee.

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:38.397
-Yes.
-A little extra pressure?

00:01:38.397 --> 00:01:42.168
-A little extra pressure.
Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.603
-But it's really --
it's a really cool show.

00:01:44.603 --> 00:01:46.806
It's a famous David Mamet play.

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:49.308
And you're doing it with Larry
Fishburne and Darren Criss.

00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:50.677
-Yeah.
-Larry Fishburne, I mean,

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:52.311
one of the --
one of the all-time greats.

00:01:52.311 --> 00:01:54.280
-Laurence Fishburne, I think,
one of the greatest

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.015
living actors
on the planet, really.

00:01:57.015 --> 00:02:00.753
He's -- He's really something.
I mean, he's -- He and I,

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:03.923
you know, we FaceTimed
during the pandemic to --

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:05.725
The lines.
We would run lines and stuff

00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:07.093
and gotten really close.

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:09.228
And the formidable Darren Criss,
who was on the "Versace."

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:11.430
-Yeah, he was fantastic.
-He did "Hedwig," you know.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.299
He's -- He's amazing, and --
Yeah.

00:02:14.299 --> 00:02:17.636
So it's -- I'm very lucky
to be working with these guys.

00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:20.373
-Do you ever -- with someone
as iconic as Laurence Fishburne,

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.141
are you ever --
are you ever looking at him

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:23.810
and seeing other characters
he's played?

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.178
-I do. I do.

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:26.645
You'll see -- Just like
you would with

00:02:26.645 --> 00:02:29.782
Christopher Walken or any of
these people, Gene Hackman,

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.751
you'll see a little
Popeye Doyle or something.

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:33.119
And, yeah, I mean,

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:34.453
you'll see a little Morpheus,
a little Ike Turner.

00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:36.088
You'll see, like, that --

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:37.623
You see -- I think with
Chris Walken, same thing.

00:02:37.623 --> 00:02:39.258
You had that experience
where you see "The Deer Hunter,"

00:02:39.258 --> 00:02:40.592
you see "The Dead Zone."

00:02:40.592 --> 00:02:42.961
And, you know, I think a lot
of people don't realize

00:02:42.961 --> 00:02:45.731
that the reason
people like Chris and Laurence

00:02:45.731 --> 00:02:49.034
are so amazing
and why they can, you know --

00:02:49.034 --> 00:02:51.771
Chris Walken in "Pulp Fiction,"
he can do that monologue

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:53.873
or, you know, all of a sudden,

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:56.142
you know,
Laurence is -- is Morpheus.

00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:58.811
I think they don't realize
all the stage they've done.

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:01.580
You know, Laurence won a Tony
for "Two Trains Running,"

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.217
and he's done "Fences"
and he's -- "Thurgood."

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:06.953
And so, like, that -- they bring
that to their film work.

00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:09.822
-Yeah. They know exactly --
Yeah, they know how to connect.

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:10.956
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:10.956 --> 00:03:12.691
-It's a really cool thing
to see.

00:03:12.691 --> 00:03:14.794
You obviously have been
a big part of the New York

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:15.962
acting scene for a long time.

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:17.397
We were talking backstage.
-Yeah.

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:19.365
-This is one of my favorite
photographs of New York City.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:20.833
-Oh, yeah, that's amazing.
-So this is --

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:22.535
And I asked you because
I never knew what it was.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:24.437
This is you, Justin Theroux,

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:26.739
Philip Seymour Hoffman,
and Billy Crudup.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:32.211
And I'm always like, "Why
are these guys in tracksuits

00:03:32.211 --> 00:03:33.880
smoking cigarettes?"

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.548
Because it doesn't
quite look like --

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:36.982
-Smoking is really --
-Yeah.

00:03:36.982 --> 00:03:38.651
-It really doesn't --

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.786
It was -- it was
a "Celebrity Charades."

00:03:40.786 --> 00:03:42.521
It's a charity.

00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:44.690
-So you were --
It was "Celebrity Charades"

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:45.858
and you dressed up in costume?

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:47.659
-Yes, and Yul Vazquez
was on that team

00:03:47.659 --> 00:03:49.027
and he took that picture.

00:03:49.027 --> 00:03:51.163
And we were the winning team
for two years in a row.

00:03:51.163 --> 00:03:54.633
And it was "Celebrity Charades,"
if you could picture that.

00:03:54.633 --> 00:03:56.836
Robin Williams did it. It was
very nice to do it for us.

00:03:56.836 --> 00:03:59.405
And a lot of people --
Julia Roberts.

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:02.208
And, so, yeah, we're just
getting ready, warming up.

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:03.576
-Yeah, you're just walking.

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:06.145
You're just on your way to
go do "Celebrity Charades."

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:07.813
-Yeah, yeah.
-Oh, so that's --

00:04:07.813 --> 00:04:09.782
-Phil did a "True West"
in the theater

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:12.118
I'm acting in now,
which is really great.

00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:14.520
-We've talked in the past
about your parents were --

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.290
Your mom was an improviser,
Your dad was an actor.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:20.159
And -- And, you know,
it must have been a unique

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:22.161
upbringing having parents
in the arts like this.

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:26.532
But what was Urban Pioneers?
-Mm!

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:30.168
Urban Pioneers -- Urban Pioneers
is kind of like Outward Bound.

00:04:30.168 --> 00:04:32.271
It's like a kind of --

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:36.609
But for me, it was for all
the sort of dropouts,

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:38.577
you know, the people
who were getting D-minuses.

00:04:38.577 --> 00:04:41.047
-Okay.
-And so they would send you

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:42.681
to this Outward Bound
kind of thing,

00:04:42.681 --> 00:04:44.783
but it turned out to be
a really amazing program.

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:46.752
-So this is -- Do they sort of
take you aside at school

00:04:46.752 --> 00:04:48.087
and they're like,
"Sam, this school is --

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:50.089
You're not crushing it.

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:52.291
So we're going to -- we're going
to send you to the woods."

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:53.859
-Yeah, exactly.
-And it was in the woods?

00:04:53.859 --> 00:04:55.361
Was it actually?
-It was in the woods.

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:57.196
We did a hiking trip
for two weeks.

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:59.498
We would rappel
off mountains and stuff,

00:04:59.498 --> 00:05:02.335
and it was really cool.
We learned how to --

00:05:02.335 --> 00:05:04.370
-Were you hesitant at first?
-I was. I was.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.272
Because all the dropouts
would go there.

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:07.840
But it turned out actually --

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.442
-Did all the dropouts come back
or did they leave a few...?

00:05:10.442 --> 00:05:11.610
-They would come back.
-Okay.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:13.178
-They would come back
more rugged.

00:05:13.178 --> 00:05:14.646
-Okay, gotcha.
-And, you know,

00:05:14.646 --> 00:05:18.017
it really, actually,
turned out to be life-changing.

00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:20.753
-Really?
-And -- Yeah, Wayne McDonald

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:23.689
was the guy, and he --
he kind of changed my life.

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:27.359
I did a monologue for the class
from "The Hustler,"

00:05:27.359 --> 00:05:29.629
and it really kind of changed
my whole direction.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:31.798
And one of the big things
is that when we went

00:05:31.798 --> 00:05:33.699
on the hiking trips,
we had to solicit food

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:35.134
from places like Pizza Hut.

00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:37.804
You have to get like five cans
of tomato sauce.

00:05:37.804 --> 00:05:39.671
-You would just walk into
a Pizza Hut and say,

00:05:39.671 --> 00:05:41.907
"We're going on a hike.
We need tomato sauce"?

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:43.242
-Exactly.
-Wow.

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:44.710
-You're, like, a teenager.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.745
And you'd go into these places,
you know -- Straw Hat Pizza.

00:05:46.745 --> 00:05:49.916
-And also, like, I mean, one of
the teenagers that looks like

00:05:49.916 --> 00:05:52.285
maybe they're not
telling the truth, right?

00:05:52.285 --> 00:05:53.552
-Exactly.

00:05:53.552 --> 00:05:55.053
-I mean, it's the dropouts,
right?

00:05:55.053 --> 00:05:57.256
-Exactly. A little shady.

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.025
And, you know, you want like,

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:02.061
you know, a case of margarine
and tomato sauce

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:03.896
for your hiking trip --
your two-week hiking trip.

00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:06.031
And they're like, "Okay, uh..."

00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:08.200
And -- But you couldn't ask
your parents to pay for it.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.802
You had to go out
and solicit it

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:12.971
and ask them to give it to you,
and that showed some initiative.

00:06:12.971 --> 00:06:16.976
If you wanted to eat on a trip,
you had to solicit this food.

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:19.812
And so that taught us
a lot about, you know, hustling

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:22.482
and, you know, auditioning
and that kind of stuff.

00:06:22.482 --> 00:06:23.849
-Yeah, that --
Actually, as an actor,

00:06:23.849 --> 00:06:25.984
it's a good idea to remember.
I mean, with that said,

00:06:25.984 --> 00:06:27.686
I don't know if I was on a hike

00:06:27.686 --> 00:06:30.656
I'd be psyched to eat, like,
a big thing of Pizza Hut butter.

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:33.326
[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.326 --> 00:06:37.563
We got a big, like, industrial
hunk of Pizza Hut margarine.

00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:40.966
-Yeah, there were some
other things and -- Yeah.

00:06:40.966 --> 00:06:42.801
It was -
Yeah, you do the best you can.

00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:43.970
-You do the best you can.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:45.704
Well, obviously --
obviously, it paid off.

00:06:45.704 --> 00:06:47.273
Whatever confidence it gave you
paid off.

00:06:47.273 --> 00:06:50.309
And now, you know, obviously
you've done a lot of film work.

00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:52.445
-Yeah.
-But being back in a theater,

00:06:52.445 --> 00:06:54.380
especially an intimate space
like that,

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.950
how is it for you to be able
to see their faces --

00:06:57.950 --> 00:06:59.985
to get that
instantaneous response?

00:06:59.985 --> 00:07:01.520
-It's amazing, you know, and --

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.657
And, you know, people they have
their masks on and stuff,

00:07:04.657 --> 00:07:07.092
but it's really --
it's really amazing.

00:07:07.092 --> 00:07:09.295
And you could see them
if they're napping

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:11.630
or if they had
their iPhones out.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:15.101
And Darren sort of gave them
a hard time at this --

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:19.972
when we asked for some money
for Broadway Cares.

00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:23.075
-Yeah.
-But, yeah, it's -- it's funny.

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:25.043
It's funny to see people
out there.

00:07:25.043 --> 00:07:27.847
-Has a -- Have you had a phone
go off of somebody --

00:07:27.847 --> 00:07:29.515
-We did.
-Okay.

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:32.919
-And Laurence maybe made them
poop their pants a little bit

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:35.187
by saying, "Don't do that."
And I think --

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:37.423
-Oh, in the middle of --
He broke -- He broke out of --

00:07:37.423 --> 00:07:40.059
-A woman went like this.

00:07:40.059 --> 00:07:43.695
And he said -- And I thought
he was going up on his lines,

00:07:43.695 --> 00:07:45.431
you know,
and I saw him on stage.

00:07:45.431 --> 00:07:48.234
And I -- I started saying my
line, and he wasn't responding.

00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:50.837
And I saw him looking out
at the house, and I realized --

00:07:50.837 --> 00:07:53.472
And then he went,
"don't do that again."

00:07:53.472 --> 00:07:55.874
And then the woman didn't.

00:07:55.874 --> 00:07:57.877
And then we were
going to complain to her,

00:07:57.877 --> 00:07:59.912
and she beat us
and complained to us

00:07:59.912 --> 00:08:01.714
about ruining her experience.

00:08:01.714 --> 00:08:03.416
-Really?
-Yes.

00:08:03.416 --> 00:08:04.650
[ Laughter ]
I think the word --

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:08.320
I know, right? It's amazing.
-Yeah.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:09.922
-I think the word "monster"
was used

00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:14.360
and that Laurence had ruined
her theatrical experience.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:17.362
And -- Wow. I mean, you know.

00:08:17.362 --> 00:08:18.798
-Yeah.
-It's something.

00:08:18.798 --> 00:08:22.067
-I do think we live in an era
where the first defense

00:08:22.067 --> 00:08:24.836
ass [bleep] use is, like,
"You're the ass [bleep]!"

00:08:24.836 --> 00:08:26.605
-Yes, yes.

00:08:26.605 --> 00:08:28.107
-And I'm really worried
about it,

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:30.008
because I feel like
that's the move these days,

00:08:30.008 --> 00:08:32.511
and I don't like it.
-I don't like it either, man.

00:08:32.511 --> 00:08:33.613
But I'll tell you what, if --

00:08:33.613 --> 00:08:35.180
if Laurie Metcalf
or Laurence Fishburne

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:37.549
told me to put my phone down,
I'd -- I'd do it.

00:08:37.549 --> 00:08:39.919
-I'd, like, put it down
and never pick it up again.

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:42.688
-Yeah, exactly. Exactly.
-Alright.

00:08:42.688 --> 00:08:44.022
Well, we're decent human beings.

00:08:44.022 --> 00:08:45.891
I think if there's anything
to take away from this.

00:08:45.891 --> 00:08:48.527
-I mean, I would hope so.
We're not at the movies.

00:08:48.527 --> 00:08:50.696
-Hey, congratulations, again.
-Hey, man, thanks a lot.

00:08:50.696 --> 00:08:52.731
-Thank you for being here.
You guys, Sam Rockwell.

00:08:52.731 --> 00:08:54.333
"American Buffalo,"

00:08:54.333 --> 00:08:56.402
now playing at the Circle
in the Square Theatre.

